[role=assistant] <DESCRIPTION>The evidence directly answers the question in Section III, “Minimum Qualifications,” of the City of Temple City’s 2020 RFP. It lists the specific certifications each proposing firm had to meet or agree to for 457 plan administration and recordkeeping services.</DESCRIPTION>
<NAME>Temple City RFP minimums.</NAME>
<CONTENT>In the City of Temple City’s 2020 RFP for 457 plan administration and recordkeeping services, each proposing firm was required to certify that it met or agreed to the following minimum qualifications:

1. The firm must have at least five (5) years of experience administering governmental Section 457 deferred compensation plans, and it must currently provide sole-provider administration to at least five (5) Section 457 deferred compensation retirement plans with assets under $25 million.

2. Any contract must provide that there will be no front-end charges or back-end charges. There also may be no restrictions or penalties on plan-initiated or participant-initiated transfers or withdrawals, including contract termination, except for:
   - capital preservation equity wash and/or put provisions related to stable value or fixed/general account products, and/or
   - mutual-fund-specific short-term trading fees.

3. The firm must accurately and fully disclose all fund expenses and all revenue-sharing arrangements associated with every fund offered in the plan. This disclosure requirement also applies to fixed or general account products, and firms offering those products must provide an accurate assessment of product expenses and revenue remitted to the firm.

4. The firm must accurately and fully disclose all expenses and revenue associated with any service made available under the plan, including services such as:
   - platform fees,
   - managed accounts,
   - investment advice,
   - financial planning, and
   - self-directed brokerage accounts.

5. The firm must agree by contract not to sell or promote products not directly affiliated with the plan unless the City gives specific written authorization.

6. Upon award of the contract, the selected firm must be duly qualified to do business in the State of California and in the City of Temple City.

7. The organization must be able to offer a self-directed brokerage option.

8. The firm must be able to transition and continue administration of existing plan loans, if needed.

9. The firm must be willing to have representatives attend City meetings in person when requested, no more than once per year.

10. The firm must know and comply with all applicable County, State of California, and federal regulations governing governmental retirement plans and investment options. The RFP also states that all applicable City and California laws, whether substantive or procedural, apply to the contract, and all relevant statutory, charter, and ordinance provisions for City contracts must be followed.

11. The terms presented throughout the RFP process, including the firm’s response and any later enhancements, must remain in place through negotiations and become part of the final contract unless the City specifically waives them in writing.

The RFP further states that by submitting a response, the proposing firm is assumed to conform to these minimum qualifications and specifications in every respect unless specifically adjusted or waived by written addendum. It also required the cover letter to acknowledge that the proposing firm meets or agrees to these minimum qualifications.</CONTENT>
[role=assistant] <SUMMARY>
# Markdown Briefing

## City of Temple City 2020 RFP: Minimum Qualifications

A proposing firm for **457 plan administration and recordkeeping services** had to certify that it met or agreed to the following minimum qualifications:

- **Relevant experience**: At least **5 years** administering governmental **Section 457 deferred compensation plans**, and current sole-provider administration for at least **5 Section 457 plans** with assets under **$25 million**.
- **No front-end or back-end charges**: Contracts could not include these charges, nor restrictions or penalties on plan- or participant-initiated transfers or withdrawals, except for limited exceptions such as:
  - equity wash/put provisions for stable value or fixed/general account products; and/or
  - mutual-fund short-term trading fees.
- **Full fee and revenue disclosure for funds**: The firm had to fully disclose **all fund expenses** and **revenue-sharing arrangements**, including for **fixed/general account products**.
- **Full disclosure for service-related expenses**: The firm had to disclose all expenses and revenue tied to plan services, including:
  - platform fees,
  - managed accounts,
  - investment advice,
  - financial planning, and
  - self-directed brokerage accounts.
- **No cross-selling without approval**: The firm had to agree not to sell or promote non-plan-affiliated products unless the **City provided written authorization**.
- **California and local qualification**: Upon award, the firm had to be **qualified to do business in California and in the City of Temple City**.
- **Self-directed brokerage capability**: The organization had to be able to offer a **self-directed brokerage option**.
- **Loan transition capability**: The firm had to be able to **transition and continue existing plan loans**, if needed.
- **In-person meeting attendance**: Representatives had to be willing to attend **City meetings in person upon request**, up to **once per year**.
- **Regulatory compliance**: The firm had to know and comply with all applicable **county, California, and federal laws and regulations** governing governmental retirement plans and investments.
- **RFP terms remain binding**: Terms in the RFP, the proposal response, and later enhancements would remain in effect through negotiations and become part of the final contract unless **waived by the City in writing**.

## Additional RFP Requirement

- By submitting a proposal, the firm was presumed to **conform to all minimum qualifications and specifications** unless modified by written addendum.
- The **cover letter** had to explicitly acknowledge that the firm **meets or agrees to these minimum qualifications**.

</SUMMARY>
